A||||6||kornholio||new tires
Z||000000||kornholio||08-23-99||07:47 PM||||I'm looking into getting new tires for my 86 SE.  The door says 215/60/14.  My question is when buying a set of tires, how important is it that I use the exact same numbers?  I'll stick with 14" tires as I have aftermarket rims.  I just want be able to drive in the rain without hydroplaning.
Z||000001||theogre||08-23-99||07:58 PM||||width of tires is not so importabt as tread design. There are several designs that provide excelent resistance to "normal" hydroplaning.<p>There are other factors as well tho that you can only partially control:<p>1. Speed.<br>2. water depth<br>3. pavement type & condition.<p>NO tire can make up for excesive speed for conditions, too much water, and poor pavement.<p>Check Consumer Reports for tire information as well as the manufacturors.
Z||000002||lowCG||08-23-99||10:50 PM||||Am going to be looking soon myself and will probably wind up getting what I had on my old car,same sizes at least,only hydroplaned a little bit and when it did it was very easy to control.<br>I used Yokahama A509s,205/60-14 on the rear,some Continental CH51 Contacts,195/65-14,on the front.I know the rolling diameter is a bit off,but not enough to upset anything.I could get a little more handling out of some wider tires,but ride,roadability,and wet handling would suffer big time.I tried some larger sizes,but it felt like a ballet dancer with logger's boots on.
Z||000003||Dale||08-24-99||02:04 AM||||You just need to get the right tire.  Width can have an affect on hydroplaning as your pressure per square inch is reduced as you go wider.  You will also find the speed rating will have an affect on your ride.  The higher the speed rating the stiffer the sidewall producing a rougher ride.  I myself try to forget about the rougher ride in trade for better performance.
Z||000004||ChadMan||08-24-99||07:45 AM||||I have a set of Firestone Firehawk SS's on my 86 SE in the original 215/60R14 size.  I think they're a really good compromise tire on a budget.  They're not the best at anything, but they're pretty good at everything: dry performance, wet performance, snow, and ride comfort.  They're a little noisy though.<p>As far as tire size, the biggest tires I ever saw on the stock 14x6 rims were 235/60R14.  They were super-fat and stuck out past the rim about an inch on either side, which I think would lead to excessive sidewall flex.  If you want bigger tires than stock, I'd recommend going with 15x7 rims. 
Z||000005||Bruce||08-24-99||06:36 PM||||Consumer Reports (March '98) did a study of touring/performance tires. The ratings:<br>1. Michelin Energy MXV4- $126<br>2. Dunlop D60 A2- $76<br>3. Goodyear Eagle GT+4- $108<br>4. Pirelli P6000- $100<br>5. BF Goodrich T/A HR4- $78<br>6. General XP 2000 H4- $70<br>7. Yokohama Avid H4- $77<br>8. Goodyear Eagle LS- $133<br>9. Bridgestone Turanza H- $98<br>10.Firestone Firehawk Touring LH- $96<br>For what it's worth, I chose the BF Goodrich because they had the best combination of price and performance. Hope that helps.<br>
Z||000006||lowCG||08-24-99||10:48 PM||||I stopped believing what Consumer Reports reports after seeing how they'd change their ratings of a comodity that is identical to what'd been tested before,say a year.<p>I try and go with who's winning races,and has the better research and developement currently.
